        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A Peloton treadmill is not one price but three, and none of them is the whole cost. The
          hardware ranges from a folding walking-focused model to a slat-belt flagship that costs
          roughly three times as much, and every one of them is sold on the assumption that you will
          also pay a monthly membership for the classes. This page sets out the listed prices on
          Peloton’s US site on 17 October 2026, what you get for them, the extras that change the
          total, and the honest alternatives if the numbers do not work for you.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How Much Is a Peloton Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>On Peloton’s US site on 17 October 2026, the Peloton Tread Flex was listed at about
            $2,195, the Tread Vision at about $3,495 and the Tread+ Vision at about $6,695, with a
            required $299 expert-assembly fee added to the Tread+ Vision at checkout.</strong> Those
            are the three treadmills Peloton described as its new range. The original Tread and
            Tread+ were not listed for sale new on that date.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of those prices includes the All-Access Membership, which Peloton says is required to
            access all of its content and features on the hardware. We do not quote the membership
            price, because it changes; check Peloton’s current figure and multiply it by the number
            of months you expect to use the machine. That second number is often the one that
            decides whether a Peloton makes sense.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are comparing a Peloton with the rest of the market, our guide to
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill costs</a>
            puts these prices in context: the Tread Flex and Tread Vision sit in the premium home
            tier, and the Tread+ Vision is priced alongside commercial-grade and slat-belt machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="current-prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Peloton Tread Prices in October 2026</h2>
<p>
            Peloton’s US treadmill page described its line-up on 17 October 2026 as three new Treads,
            and independent launch coverage in late September 2026 reported the same three models at
            the same prices, on sale from 1 October. Here is what each was listed at, rounded as
            Peloton showed them.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it is</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Listed price, 17 Oct 2026</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Assembly</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tread Flex</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Peloton’s folding treadmill</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $2,195</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Self-assembly, or paid expert assembly</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tread Vision</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cushioned-belt Tread with a movement-tracking camera</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $3,495</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Self-assembly, or paid expert assembly</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tread+ Vision</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slat-belt flagship with a movement-tracking camera</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $6,695</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Expert assembly required: $299, for about $6,994 in total</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Three things to keep in mind when reading those figures. First, they are list prices on
            one day; Peloton has changed its treadmill prices several times since the first Tread
            went on sale, and promotions come and go, so the figure on the day you buy may be different in either direction.
            Second, sales tax and any accessories are extra. Third, the membership is separate from
            all three.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you searched for the "Peloton Tread price" or the "Peloton Tread+ price" expecting the
            older models, the closest current equivalents are the Tread Vision and the Tread+ Vision.
            The older machines still appear on the used market, and we cover what they were sold for
            in the section on used Pelotons below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tread-vs-tread-plus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Tread vs Tread+: What the Extra Money Buys</h2>
<p>
            We deliberately avoid quoting motor, belt or capacity specifications on price pages, so
            this is a description of what separates the three models in Peloton’s own words, not a
            spec sheet. Peloton’s comparison page is the place to check the measurements before you
            buy, particularly the space and ceiling requirements.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tread Flex</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton describes the Tread Flex as its folding treadmill, for people who want to stow and
            move the machine. It lacks the movement-tracking camera of the other two, so the form and
            rep-tracking features Peloton advertises are not available on it. Independent launch
            coverage described it as aimed more at walking and lower-impact training than hard
            running. If you mostly walk, our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill guide</a>
            covers the wider field at this price and below.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tread Vision</h3>
          <p>
            The Tread Vision is the mid-priced model: a cushioned belt, a swivelling touchscreen and
            the movement-tracking camera that Peloton uses for its run-analysis and strength
            features. For most people considering "a Peloton treadmill", this is the one being
            compared with premium machines from other brands.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tread+ Vision</h3>
          <p>
            The Tread+ Vision is the flagship. Its running surface is a slat belt — rubberised slats
            rather than a continuous belt over a deck — and Peloton lists a Free Mode, in which the
            motor is switched off and you drive the belt yourself, and a Sled Mode that adds
            resistance for sled-push style work. It is larger and heavier than the Tread Vision and
            requires expert assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The honest trade-off.</strong> Roughly $3,200 separates the Tread Vision from the
            Tread+ Vision at list price. That buys the slat belt and the self-powered modes, not more
            classes: the membership and class library are the same. If the slat-belt feel is the
            attraction, it is worth comparing with other slat and curved designs; our
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a>
            covers the self-powered alternatives.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who each one suits</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Tread Flex:</strong> walkers and light joggers who want Peloton classes and
            need to fold the machine away, and who do not care about the camera features.</li>
            <li><strong>Tread Vision:</strong> runners who want the full Peloton class experience,
            including the camera-based form and strength features, without the flagship price.</li>
            <li><strong>Tread+ Vision:</strong> people who specifically want a slat belt, the
            self-powered Free Mode or Sled Mode, and have the space, floor and budget for a much
            larger machine.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are unsure between the first two, the deciding questions are usually space and
            the camera. If you are unsure between the last two, the deciding question is whether you
            will use the slat-belt modes often enough to justify the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whats-included" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Is Included: Delivery, Assembly, Trial and Warranty</h2>
<p>
            Peloton’s shop pages on 17 October 2026 set out the following. Terms change, so treat this
            as a checklist of things to confirm rather than a guarantee.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Delivery.</strong> Peloton listed a free delivery option for the Tread Flex and
            Tread Vision. Self-assembly includes delivery to the room of your choice but not unpacking
            or assembly, and Peloton warned that the boxes are heavy and may need two or more people.
            Remote locations may not be served, and extra delivery charges may apply outside the
            continental US.</li>
            <li><strong>Assembly.</strong> Expert assembly was an optional paid extra on the Tread Flex
            and Tread Vision and a required $299 fee on the Tread+ Vision. Peloton describes expert
            assembly as delivery to your room, unpacking, inspection for damage, assembly and removal
            of packaging. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery</a>
            explains what to check on the day.</li>
            <li><strong>Home trial.</strong> Peloton offered a 30-day home trial for first-time buyers,
            one per household, in the 48 contiguous states. Payment is up front, and return fees
            apply, so a trial return is not free.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty.</strong> The Tread Flex and Tread Vision were listed with a 12-month
            limited warranty plus longer guarantees on the belt, motor and frame. Peloton’s pages gave
            two different belt-and-motor terms for the Tread+ Vision, so read the warranty document
            for the model you are buying. Peloton also says the warranty is void if the Tread is
            moved outside the US, and it sells an optional protection plan.</li>
            <li><strong>Old equipment removal.</strong> Peloton offered paid haul-away of an old
            treadmill or other equipment through a third-party provider, in most of the continental
            US.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Before ordering, check the space and electrical requirements on Peloton’s product page:
            it specifies clearance around the machine, a minimum ceiling height relative to the
            user, and a dedicated, grounded circuit. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill space guide</a>
            explains why the clearance behind the belt matters most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Membership: What It Unlocks and What Works Without It</h2>
<p>
            Every Peloton treadmill listing on 17 October 2026 carried the line "All-Access Membership
            separate", and Peloton states that the membership is required to access all Peloton
            content and applicable features on your hardware. We do not quote the price, because it
            has changed before and may change again. Check Peloton’s current figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the membership covers, according to Peloton.</strong> The live and on-demand
            class library — running, walking, strength, bootcamp, yoga and more — on the treadmill and
            the Peloton App, Scenic routes, training programmes and challenges. One membership covers
            the whole household, with individual profiles. It covers one Peloton product of each type,
            so a household with a Peloton bike and a Peloton treadmill needs one membership, but two
            treadmills need two.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Moving between Peloton products.</strong> Peloton says your membership stays the
            same across products, so if you already pay for one with a Peloton bike, adding a Tread
            does not need a second membership — unless it is a second treadmill. That changes the
            sums considerably for existing Peloton households, because the extra running cost of a
            Tread is then close to nothing beyond the hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What works without it.</strong> Peloton says you do not need a membership to use
            its Just Run feature on the Tread Vision, which lets you set speed and incline yourself.
            Most of what you are paying the hardware premium for, however — the classes, the
            leaderboard and the camera-based features — depends on the membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The arithmetic that matters.</strong> Take the current monthly membership price,
            multiply it by the months you realistically expect to use the treadmill, and add it to
            the hardware price. Over several years the membership can approach or exceed the cost of
            the machine. Our guide to
            <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton</a>
            compares the two main subscription ecosystems, including what each treadmill does
            without its subscription.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="financing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Financing, HSA/FSA and the Real Monthly Cost</h2>
<p>
            Peloton’s US pages advertised financing through Affirm, shown as a 12-month plan, with the
            rate set by eligibility — 0% APR for some buyers and a higher rate for others — and a
            down payment that may be required. The monthly figure shown next to each treadmill is the
            hardware price divided over that term at 0%; it does not include the membership, tax or
            accessories.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three points are worth holding on to:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The monthly hardware figure is not the monthly cost.</strong> Add the
            membership to it. For the first year you are paying both; after the plan ends, the
            membership continues for as long as you want the classes.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the actual offer at checkout.</strong> Whether you get 0% or a higher
            rate, and whether a down payment is needed, is decided when you apply. The disclosure is
            the authority, not the banner.</li>
            <li><strong>HSA/FSA is a maybe, not a promise.</strong> Peloton offered a pay-with-HSA/FSA
            option through a third party, Truemed, and its own footnote says qualification and
            reimbursement are not guaranteed. Our guide to whether
            <a href="/are-treadmills-hsa-fsa-eligible/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills are HSA or FSA eligible</a>
            explains how that process generally works.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For the general mechanics of 0% plans, deferred interest and subscription bundles, our
            <a href="/treadmill-financing/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill financing guide</a>
            works through the arithmetic with round-number examples.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-refurbished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Refurbished and Used Peloton Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Refurbished from Peloton.</strong> On 17 October 2026 Peloton’s US Certified
            Refurbished page offered refurbished bikes only. We found no refurbished Peloton treadmill
            listed by Peloton in the US on that date. If one appears, check whether it carries the
            same warranty as a new machine and whether the home trial applies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Used from a private seller.</strong> Older Peloton treadmills turn up on local
            marketplaces. For context on what they cost new, the Consumer Product Safety Commission’s
            recall notices state that the original Tread+ (model TR01, sold as the "Peloton Tread"
            from 2018 and renamed Tread+ in September 2020) sold for about $4,295, and that the
            original Tread (model TR02) had a limited US release for about $2,495. A used price should
            reflect the machine’s age, its condition and the fact that it has no transferable home
            trial.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What a fair used price looks like.</strong> There is no official guide price for
            a used Peloton treadmill, so judge it the way you would any used premium machine: start
            from what it originally cost, take off a meaningful amount for every year of use, and
            then take off more for anything that shifts cost onto you — collection and moving, the
            activation fee, missing parts, worn belts or slats, and the absence of a warranty. A
            low price on a recalled model that has not had its repair is not a bargain at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four checks matter more for a used Peloton than for most used treadmills:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Recall status.</strong> Get the model number from the sticker on the front of
            the deck. A TR01 Tread+ or TR02 Tread falls under the 2021 recalls described below. Ask
            whether the Tread+ rear guard has been fitted and whether the TR02 touchscreen repair was
            done, and check with Peloton before you pay.</li>
            <li><strong>The membership still applies.</strong> A used Peloton needs the same
            membership for its classes. Peloton has also introduced a one-time activation fee for
            used equipment bought from a previous owner; check the current amount on Peloton’s
            support site.</li>
            <li><strong>A factory reset.</strong> Ask the seller to remove their account and reset
            the machine before handover.</li>
            <li><strong>Moving it.</strong> Peloton treadmills are heavy, and slat-belt models
            especially so. Price in professional movers if you cannot collect it safely.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our guide on
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a used treadmill</a>
            covers sources and safe meetings, and the
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers the inspection itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recall-history" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. The Tread+ Recall History</h2>
<p>
            Peloton’s treadmill history includes one of the most serious home treadmill recalls of
            recent years, and anyone pricing a used Tread+ should know it. Everything below is taken
            from the Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall records and news releases.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">May 2021: the Tread+ recall</h3>
          <p>
            On 5 May 2021 Peloton recalled about 125,000 Tread+ treadmills (model TR01) because adult
            users, children, pets and objects could be pulled underneath the rear of the treadmill,
            posing a risk of injury or death. The notice reported that a six-year-old child had died
            after being pulled under the rear, and that Peloton had received 72 reports of people,
            pets or objects being pulled under, including 29 reports of injuries to children. Owners
            were told to stop using the treadmill and contact Peloton; the remedies included a
            refund, a free move to a room children and pets cannot reach, and a software update that
            locks the Tread+ after each use behind a four-digit passcode.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">May 2021: the Tread recall</h3>
          <p>
            On the same day, Peloton recalled the original Tread (model TR02), a separate machine,
            because its touchscreen could detach and fall. The CPSC notice covered about 1,050 units
            in the US, with more in Canada, and the remedy was a refund or a free repair visit to
            secure the touchscreen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">January 2023: the civil penalty</h3>
          <p>
            On 5 January 2023 the CPSC announced that Peloton had agreed to pay a $19,065,000 civil
            penalty, over charges that it knowingly failed to report the Tread+ hazard immediately
            and that it distributed recalled Tread+ treadmills after the recall was announced.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">May 2023: the rear guard repair</h3>
          <p>
            On 18 May 2023 the CPSC and Peloton announced an approved rear guard repair for recalled
            Tread+ treadmills, with a breakaway design that pivots away when it contacts a person or
            object, cutting power and slowing the belt. The guard and installation were free. The
            release headline put the total at one child death and 90 injuries reported. Peloton’s
            safety page on 17 October 2026 said the rear safety guard is available for Tread+ models
            purchased before May 2021, through Member Support.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What this means for you.</strong> If you own or are buying a TR01 Tread+, confirm
            the guard has been fitted. With any treadmill, keep children and pets away while it is in
            use, remove the safety key afterwards and store it out of reach — Peloton’s safety page
            says the same. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety guide</a>
            covers household precautions in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="turn-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. How to Turn On a Peloton Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The short version is the same as for most treadmills: plug the Tread into a suitable wall
            outlet, switch on the main power switch on the machine itself, clip the safety key into
            its slot, and wait for the touchscreen to start up. Peloton specifies a dedicated,
            grounded circuit for its current Treads, and its support guidance warns against power
            strips, adapters and extension cords.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the screen stays dark, check the main switch first, then the outlet, then the safety
            key, which must be in place before the belt will run. On connected treadmills a dark
            screen is often a console that is asleep or still booting rather than a machine with no
            power. Because switch positions and start controls differ between Peloton generations,
            the manual and setup guide for your model are the authority.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the full sequence, including where power switches usually sit, what standby looks
            like and what to try when nothing happens, see our step-by-step guide on
            <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to turn on a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If it asks for a passcode.</strong> Peloton’s safety page describes Tread Lock, a
            passcode that guards against unauthorised use whenever the Tread is idle. If a Peloton
            treadmill starts up but will not let you begin a workout, check whether Tread Lock is on
            and enter the household’s passcode. On a used machine, ask the seller to remove
            their account and passcode before handover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Alternatives to a Peloton Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            A Peloton makes most sense for someone who specifically wants Peloton’s instructors and
            class format and is happy to pay for the membership long term. If that is not you, these
            are the realistic alternatives.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Another connected treadmill.</strong> Other brands sell treadmills built
            around their own class apps, at a wide range of prices, and most of them also tie their
            headline features to a subscription. Our
            <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton comparison</a>
            covers the main rival ecosystem, and our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmill guide</a>
            compares the field.</li>
            <li><strong>A treadmill without a subscription.</strong> A well-built manual-control
            treadmill and a free or low-cost app on a tablet can cover most training needs. Our list
            of the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            starts there.</li>
            <li><strong>The Peloton App on another treadmill.</strong> Peloton’s app gives access to
            its classes under its own app memberships. On a non-Peloton treadmill you adjust speed
            and incline yourself to follow the instructor’s cues, which is a reasonable way to find
            out whether you like the classes before spending thousands on the hardware.</li>
            <li><strong>A slat or curved treadmill.</strong> If the Tread+ Vision appeals for its slat
            belt or self-powered mode, curved manual treadmills cover similar ground for interval
            and sprint work, without a membership.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a broader starting point, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills</a>
            roundup covers the strongest machines across price tiers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Is a Peloton Treadmill Worth the Price?</h2>
<p>
            It depends almost entirely on whether you will use the classes. Strip out the membership,
            and you are paying a premium-tier price for a treadmill whose main advantage is the
            software ecosystem it was built around. Keep the membership, and you are paying that
            price plus a monthly fee for a training format some people find genuinely motivating.
          </p>
          <p>
            A Peloton tends to be a reasonable purchase when you already know you like Peloton’s
            classes — from the app, a friend’s machine or a studio — and you have budgeted for the
            membership over several years. It tends to be a poor purchase when the classes are a
            maybe, when the monthly figure is what makes it feel affordable, or when you mainly walk
            and would be served by a machine at a fraction of the price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A simple way to do the sum.</strong> Write down the hardware price, any assembly
            fee and your sales tax. Add twelve months of the current membership for the first year.
            Then, for every further year you expect to keep the machine, add another twelve months of
            membership. For the Tread Vision at its 17 October 2026 list price, the first-year
            figure is $3,495 plus tax plus a year of membership; by year five, it is the same hardware
            plus five years of membership. Run the same sum for the alternative you are considering,
            with its own subscription if it has one, and compare like with like.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before ordering, work out three numbers: the delivered hardware price including any
            assembly fee and tax, the membership over the years you expect to keep the machine, and
            the price of the best non-Peloton alternative that would meet the same need. If the
            first two together still feel right against the third, the Peloton is a considered
            choice rather than an impulse.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


